\section{Compute Layout for One Target, Not for C in General}

An FFI field descriptor needs a byte offset, a native representation
and an alignment constraint. To see why size alone is not enough,
define a C record with members \texttt{uint8\_t kind},
\texttt{uint32\_t count}, \texttt{double mean}, and
\texttt{uint16\_t flags}, in that order. Under the ordinary LP64 System
V AMD64 layout in the local supplement, an aggregate has alignment
sufficient for its most strictly aligned member. Place each member at
the first available appropriately aligned offset, and round the final
extent to that aggregate alignment. The result is a record with the
following arrangement.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrl}
\hline
Region & First byte & Bytes & Reason \\
\hline
\texttt{kind} & 0 & 1 & Byte member \\
Padding & 1 & 3 & Align \texttt{count} to 4 \\
\texttt{count} & 4 & 4 & Four-byte member \\
\texttt{mean} & 8 & 8 & Eight-byte aligned member \\
\texttt{flags} & 16 & 2 & Two-byte member \\
Tail padding & 18 & 6 & Round stride to 24 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

An array of two records puts the second \texttt{mean} at byte 32,
not at byte 26. The size recorded in a binding must be usable as
the stride; summing visible fields does not even account for the
first record's internal gap. Some headers deliberately change
alignment using compiler directives. Such attributes are part of
the configured declaration, not conventions inferred from field
names. The Common Lisp extract's discussions of foreign compound
types and foreign storage distinguish modeling a layout from
allocating and managing actual memory of that type.

\section{Distinguish a Memory View from a Call Classification}

Memory layout specifies where the bytes reside when an object exists
in memory, not how an object passed by value travels. Under System V
AMD64 a plain record containing one \texttt{double} followed by one
\texttt{uint64\_t} can be split into an SSE eightbyte and an INTEGER
eightbyte. Register exhaustion can force the entire argument to memory
instead of allocating just the part for which a register was
available. Passing a pointer to that record would be a single pointer
argument. Passing the larger record from the table by value is yet
another case: under the cited convention its ordinary fields do not
satisfy the vector-class exception for larger objects.

Other ABIs may use a different classification even when their memory
layout matches. For an early implementation prefer a C shim taking
a pointer to input and output records, compiled against the real
header. The generated language view then needs layout only for the
parts it accesses. If a C function returns a record via hidden
caller-provided storage, that detail stays within the compiled shim.
\Cref{fig:visual-388} shows why a successful offset test is not a
successful call test.

