\section{Verify Addressable Fields and Delegate Bit-Fields}

\begin{verbatim}
verify_target(layout, c_probe):
    require_equal(layout.size, c_probe.sizeof_record())
    require_equal(layout.align, c_probe.alignof_record())
    for field in layout.addressable_members:
        require_equal(field.offset, c_probe.offsetof(field.name))
    for field in layout.bit_fields:
        require(field.supported_getter_symbol)
        require(field.supported_setter_symbol)

probe_get_mode(record):
    return record.mode        # compiled against native header

probe_set_mode(record, value):
    if not 0 <= value <= 7: return RANGE
    record.mode := value      # hypothetical unsigned three-bit field
    return OK
\end{verbatim}

The bit-field helper is target C code expressed as pseudocode.
Ordinary member addresses and \texttt{offsetof} will not give a
bit-field offset. The ABI extract describes a concrete allocation
policy, but byte order alone does not specify a portable one.
Allocation unit, direction, rules for crossing or ending a unit and
field signedness can matter. Test that setting mode to \(5\) after
setting a neighboring field does not change the neighbor. Reject
out-of-range setter values rather than trusting truncation to reflect
the public operation. If clients need a portable serialized format,
write an explicit bit encoding for that format, not for the
compiler's C record.

\section{Expose a Tagged View of a Union}

A union overlays its alternatives at the same location and has room
and alignment for the supported members. A union of a double and a
32-bit integer in the example target is aligned for the double and
occupies eight bytes. Neither an address nor the extent identifies
which member the supplier intended. Suppose the record containing
the union also holds a separate kind field: the wrapper should
decode that kind first and read only the corresponding member, or
report an unknown variant without examining the payload. Do not
treat arbitrary inactive bytes as a second public value.

Verify layout with a native fixture that writes distinct values,
followed by one in which the language writes and C reads. Include
arrays and records nested inside larger records so stride and
alignment errors cannot hide at offset zero. Compare fields, not
the padding bytes: padding has no promised semantic value and is
not a stable basis for equality, hashing or interchange. Keep the
fixture built with the library's packing switches and target defines;
testing the default layout of a different translation unit can
certify a perfectly consistent but irrelevant representation.

\section{Work Through a Packed Extension Without Generalizing It}

Suppose a target compiler is configured with a packing extension
that caps every member's effective alignment at one for a
specific record. Applied to the four members in the table,
this configuration yields offsets \(0,1,5,13\), total size
\(15\) and aggregate alignment \(1\). The second record's
\texttt{mean} would begin at byte \(20\), not \(32\).
An aligned base allocation would not make the first
\texttt{mean} aligned: its byte offset is five. A runtime
decoder can copy exactly eight bytes from that offset into
an aligned native double and then decode that double under
the target representation. Casting the original address to
a native double pointer and dereferencing it is not an
equivalent portable operation.

This packing can change the machine classification too.
The System V aggregate classification includes a MEMORY
case for unaligned fields. Using the ordinary record's
layout table with just an adjusted stride is therefore not
enough. Neither does the example justify applying a unit
alignment cap to every compiler feature with ``packed''
in its name: explicit alignment attributes, nested records
and individual member attributes may interact differently.
Ask the configured compiler, or generate accessors so the
binding does not need to represent the extension at all.

\begin{verbatim}
decode_packed_mean(view, record_index, layout):
    require(layout.target == active_library_target())
    require(0 <= record_index < view.record_count)
    base := checked_multiply(record_index, layout.size)
    address := checked_add(base, layout.offset_of_mean)
    require(contains_bytes(view, address, native_sizeof_double))
    tmp := allocate_aligned_native_double()
    copy_bytes(tmp, view, address, native_sizeof_double)
    return decode_target_double(tmp)
\end{verbatim}

This helper is for a live native memory view, not for loading
records from a file. A file may specify an endian or floating
format unrelated to the runtime target, and its length may
represent no valid record sequence. Verify an unaligned field
with a distinct pattern in adjacent fields: that exposes an
implementation that rounds the field address down to the
nearest aligned position before copying. Check count and
extent separately; a stale metadata count must not authorize
a read beyond the actual view.

For a versioned record with a size field, consider whether
the supplier guarantees a common readable prefix. If it
does, an older library may supply that prefix and omit
later fields. Do not use the size field as an array stride
unless the API specifically defines it that way. A supplied
record can be smaller than the allocation that contains it,
and conversely the actual extent limits what may be read
even when a version claims a larger type.
